[size=5][b]Smoothwalker - Third Person Camera[/b][/size]

Smoothwalker is a C++ mod for UE4SS. It removes the game's camera lag and replaces it with its own smoothing.

Three presets ship with it: Tight, Balanced, and Cinematic. Cinematic is the only one that also smooths turning. Distance, height, shoulder offset, and FOV can each be set per camera mode, and V swaps shoulders.

This is an early build. The camera works as intended, performance is fine, and I've kept it as compatible as I could, but I have not spent many hours of real gameplay on it yet. The presets feel good to me, though I am still tweaking them. Everything is adjustable, so if you end up making your own, I'd be curious what you come up with.


[youtube]SRa63f47Hnw[/youtube]

[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]UE4SS, either one:
[list]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/thebloodofdawnwalker/mods/18]UE4SS for Dawnwalker[/url] by Vercadi, file Dawnwalker-UE4SS-v1.2.1-rc6-build25232147 (version 1.3 on that page).[/*]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/thebloodofdawnwalker/mods/283]UE4SS for BoD[/url] by Framecore, version 2c or 2b, Performance or Compatibility profile.[/*]
[/list]
[/*]
[*]The Steam version of the game, build 1.0.5 (25232147). On any other build, the mod stays inactive and states this in [font=Courier New]UE4SS.log[/font].[/*]
[*]Optional: [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/thebloodofdawnwalker/mods/271]Mod Setting Menu[/url] for the in-game settings page.[/*]
[/list]

[size=4][b]Installation[/b][/size]
Extract the archive into the folder that holds the [i]ue4ss[/i] folder and Dawnwalker.exe:
[code]<Steam library>\steamapps\common\The Blood of Dawnwalker\Dawnwalker\Binaries\Win64[/code]
No mods.txt edit needed. [font=Courier New]UE4SS.log[/font] shows these two lines once it has loaded:
[code][DWSmoothwalker] v0.10.0 loaded, on, 0 saved slots, 0 presets from the presets folder
[DWSmoothwalker] GetCameraView hooked (slot 214)[/code]
To uninstall, delete ue4ss\Mods\DWSmoothwalker. Nothing is written to save games.

[b]Updating:[/b] Close the game and extract over the old folder. The archive replaces [font=Courier New]config\smoothwalker.ini[/font], so save your settings to a slot first, or keep a copy of the file and put it back afterwards: keys new to this version are added to it at the next start. Slots and installed presets survive.

[b][size=4]Features[/size][/b]
[list]
[*]A smoother third-person camera that replaces the game's stiff camera lag.[/*]
[*]Three built-in presets, Tight, Balanced, and Cinematic, that you can cycle with a hotkey.[/*]
[*]Distance, height, shoulder offset, and FOV are adjustable per situation (exploring, sprinting, combat, focus, aiming, traversal), with a shoulder swap key (V).[/*]
[*]Settings can be changed in the in-game Mod Setting Menu (optional) or a plain ini file.[/*]
[*]A preset is a small ini file with 41 keys, placed in config/presets.[/*]
[*]A template with every key and its default is included in the optional files.[/*]
[*]Six in-game save slots that work as profiles: pick one, tune, and every change is saved into it. Export it when you're done.[/*]
[*]Follow in combat and in traversal (claw ride, anti-grav, shadowstep): how much of the trail, and of the turning smoothing, stays while those cameras are up. Aiming has had this since the first release.[/*]
[*]Plays along with mods that change the game's camera modes: a distance, FOV or look limit another mod writes is kept, Smoothwalker's settings apply on top, and one log line says which mode and value.[/*]
[*]A camera API for other UE4SS Lua mods: read the camera, add an offset, rotation or FOV layer, or take the camera over for a while and hand it back. See the mod authors section below.[/*]
[*]A debug overlay (Debug group on the menu page, or [font=Courier New]debug_key[/font]) that shows the live camera state at the top right of the screen, for tuning presets and for bug reports.[/*]
[/list]
[size=4][b]Known issues[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]After loading a preset from the Mod Menu, the open page continues to show the old slider values until you reopen it.[/*]
[*]The debug overlay is not hidden by photo mode or a hidden HUD. Switch it off for screenshots.[/*]
[/list]

[size=4][b]Compatibility[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Another mod that replaces the third-person camera update itself would fight it. Run one or the other, unless that mod uses the camera API to take the camera over.[/*]
[*]Mods that only change the game's camera modes (distance, FOV, look limits) work alongside it: Smoothwalker keeps their values and applies your settings on top.[/*]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/thebloodofdawnwalker/mods/350]FreeCam - UE4SS[/url] ([font=Courier New]F7[/font]) works alongside it. Leaving the free camera snaps the game camera back to your character.[/*]
[/list]

[size=4][b]Performance[/b][/size]
The camera math runs once per frame inside the game's own camera update. Measured cost is a few microseconds, with no measurable frame-time difference and no stutters.

[size=4][b]Presets and keys[/b][/size]
[spoiler]
[list]
[*][font=Courier New]V[/font]: swap shoulders. The camera glides across in half a second.[/*]
[*]Presets: pick one on the Mod Settings page or with [font=Courier New]preset[/font] in the ini. [b]Tight[/b] barely trails, camera a little closer. [b]Balanced[/b] (default) trails clearly and settles fast. [b]Cinematic[/b] trails heavily, smooths turning, sits further back and higher, and widens the view when you sprint. After those come your six save slots, then any presets installed from other authors.[/*]
[*]Save slots: pick a slot marked [b](empty)[/b] and your current settings are saved into it. While that slot is shown, every Apply saves into it too, so you can keep tuning. Pick Custom or another preset to stop. Built-in presets and installed ones never change.[/*]
[*]On and off: the [font=Courier New]enabled[/font] switch, same page or ini. Off is the game's own camera, untouched.[/*]
[/list]
Only [font=Courier New]V[/font] is bound by default, and the game leaves that key free. To cycle presets, toggle the mod or show the debug overlay from a key, set [font=Courier New]preset_key[/font], [font=Courier New]toggle_key[/font] or [font=Courier New]debug_key[/font] in the ini. A banner at the left of the screen confirms preset changes and on/off.

A preset carries most of the camera settings; a few can't be changed per preset yet (see the Settings spoiler).

[b]With Mod Setting Menu.[/b] Smoothwalker gets its own page under Mod Settings, in both the main and pause menus. It covers everything except the four key bindings and [font=Courier New]log_verbose[/font]. The Preset entry at the top shows which preset your settings match, or Custom. Pick another, press Apply, and it's live within a quarter second. No restart.

[b]Without it.[/b] Edit [font=Courier New]config\smoothwalker.ini[/font] in the mod folder. Save, tab back in, and the change is live within a quarter second. Key bindings are the exception: they're read at game start.[/spoiler]

[size=4][b]Settings[/b][/size] ([font=Courier New]config\smoothwalker.ini[/font] in the mod folder)
[spoiler]
[b]Keys[/b] (read at game start; any UE4SS key name)
[list]
[*][font=Courier New]toggle_key =[/font], [font=Courier New]preset_key =[/font], [font=Courier New]debug_key =[/font] (blank, no key), [font=Courier New]shoulder_key = V[/font][/*]
[/list]
[b]Follow[/b] (in presets, except enabled and wall_clamp)
[list]
[*][font=Courier New]enabled = 1[/font]: 0 is the game's own camera: no follow, no camera position changes, and the preset and shoulder keys do nothing.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]follow_rate_h = 6.5[/font], [font=Courier New]follow_rate_v = 10[/font]: catch-up rate per second, horizontal and vertical. Higher is tighter.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]curve_h = 2[/font], [font=Courier New]curve_v = 0[/font]: 0 constant, 1 linear, 2 smoothstep, 3 ease in-out. The curves start gently and reach full rate at [font=Courier New]catchup_distance = 150[/font] cm; [font=Courier New]min_rate_scale = 0.35[/font] is the rate at zero lag.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]max_lag_h = 85[/font], [font=Courier New]max_lag_v = 50[/font]: the furthest the camera may fall behind, in cm. 0 turns that axis's trail off, and its follow rate and curve then do nothing.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]soft_leash = 1[/font]: eases into the lag limit instead of stopping hard at it.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]aiming_follow = 30[/font]: percent of the trail and turning smoothing kept while you aim. 0 locks the camera to your aim, 100 trails as everywhere else.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]combat_follow = 100[/font]: percent of the trail kept while the combat camera is up. 0 locks the camera to your character, 100 trails as everywhere else. Eases in and out; while you aim, aiming_follow applies instead.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]traversal_follow = 100[/font]: percent of the trail kept during claw ride, anti-grav and shadowstep. 0 locks the camera to your character, 100 trails as everywhere else. Eases in and out; while you aim, aiming_follow applies instead, and in combat, combat_follow.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]rotation_smoothing = 0[/font], [font=Courier New]rotation_rate = 20[/font]: smooth turning too, and how fast it catches up. The smoothed turn trails by at most a quarter turn and always catches up the way you turned.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]combat_rotation = 100[/font]: percent of the turning smoothing kept while the combat camera is up. Only matters with rotation_smoothing on; while you aim, aiming_follow applies instead.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]traversal_rotation = 100[/font]: percent of the turning smoothing kept during claw ride, anti-grav and shadowstep. Only matters with rotation_smoothing on; while you aim, aiming_follow applies instead, and in combat, combat_rotation.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]wall_clamp = 1[/font]: keeps the smoothed camera in front of walls the game pulled its camera in for.[/*]
[/list]
[b]Safety and display[/b] (not in presets)
[list]
[*][font=Courier New]reset_distance = 500[/font]: cm moved in one frame that counts as a teleport and snaps the camera.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]reset_gap = 0.25[/font]: seconds without camera updates (cutscene, free camera, load) before a snap.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]show_banner = 1[/font]: the preset and on/off banner.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]log_stats = 0[/font]: 1 writes smoothing statistics and the per-frame cost to [font=Courier New]UE4SS.log[/font] every 5 seconds.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]log_verbose = 0[/font]: 1 adds detail to [font=Courier New]UE4SS.log[/font]: player discovery, offsets, camera mode layouts, combat and traversal camera changes. Not on the menu page.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]debug_overlay = 0[/font]: 1 shows a panel at the top right of the screen, refreshed four times a second: on or off, the preset, the game's camera type and modes, how much of the trail is kept and why, the lag, the last snap and whether another mod holds the camera. [font=Courier New]debug_key[/font] flips it in game.[/*]
[/list]
[b]Camera position[/b] (100 / 0 / 0 / 0 is the game as shipped; in presets except camera_tuning, shoulder_swap and position_transition)
[list]
[*][font=Courier New]camera_tuning = 1[/font]: 0 puts every camera mode back as the game ships it.[/*]
[*]Per group, with the prefix [font=Courier New]exploration_[/font], [font=Courier New]sprint_[/font], [font=Courier New]combat_[/font], [font=Courier New]focus_[/font], [font=Courier New]aiming_[/font] or [font=Courier New]traversal_[/font]: [font=Courier New]distance[/font] (% of the game's, 25 to 250), [font=Courier New]height[/font] (cm), [font=Courier New]shoulder[/font] (cm further out; centered cameras stay centered; not for traversal), [font=Courier New]fov[/font] (degrees added).[/*]
[*]Focus has its own group since 0.9.0. Settings files and presets from before take their combat values for focus until you change them.[/*]
[*]The game's own camera lag is switched off while Smoothwalker is on, so the follow settings above are the only lag. Off puts it back.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]shoulder_swap = 0[/font]: 1 is the other shoulder. [font=Courier New]V[/font] switches it.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]position_transition = 0.5[/font]: seconds a position change or shoulder swap glides over. 0 snaps.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]pitch_min = -60[/font], [font=Courier New]pitch_max = 40[/font]: look down and up limits in the modes the game ships at -60 / 40: exploring, sprinting, focus and the shadowstep base. Aiming, combat, claw ride and anti-grav already allow -89 / 89 and keep their own.[/*]
[/list]
[b]Presets[/b]: [font=Courier New]preset = 102[/font] shows the matching preset (0 Custom, 101 Tight, 102 Balanced, 103 Cinematic, 1 to 6 a slot, 201 and up an installed preset); change it to load one. Set it to a slot you have not used yet and the mod saves your current settings into that slot instead; with a saved slot set, every later change is saved into it too. The mod writes this, and key presses that change it, back to the ini only while you play, never behind an open Mod Menu page. Slots go to [font=Courier New]config\presets\Slot N.ini[/font]; the mod creates the folder.[/spoiler]

[size=4][b]For mod authors: making a preset[/b][/size]
[spoiler]
If you land on a camera you like, share it. The optional file [b]Smoothwalker Example Preset[/b] (Files tab) installs one preset, Over the Shoulder, with every key commented: range, default and what it does. It's the quickest template to copy.

A preset is one [b].ini[/b] file in [i]ue4ss\Mods\DWSmoothwalker\config\presets[/i]. Smoothwalker reads that folder at game start, adds each file to the preset cycle after the player's own slots, and lists it by name in the Preset entry on the Mod Menu page.

[b]1)[/b] Set the camera up in game, from the Mod Menu or the ini.
[b]2)[/b] On the Smoothwalker page, set Preset to a slot marked [b](empty)[/b] and press Apply. This writes [i]presets\Slot N.ini[/i] with every setting a preset carries. Leave that slot picked and every further Apply saves your changes into it, so you can keep tuning.
[b]3)[/b] Copy that file under a name of your own, for example [i]presets\YourName - Low Chase.ini[/i]. Don't use [b]Slot 1.ini[/b] to [b]Slot 6.ini[/b]: those are the player's save slots and the mod overwrites them.
[b]4)[/b] Change the [b]name[/b] line. That's what the banner and the Mod Menu show. Comments starting with [b];[/b] stay in the file; the mod never rewrites a preset it didn't save.
[b]5)[/b] Restart the game. The preset appears after the slots.

[code]; Low Chase by YourName
name = Low Chase
follow_rate_h = 5
follow_rate_v = 6
max_lag_h = 110
exploration_distance = 120
exploration_height = -20
exploration_fov = 5[/code]

[b]Rules[/b]
[list]
[*]One [b]key = value[/b] per line. A preset may list only the keys it wants to change; loading it leaves every other setting as the player has it.[/*]
[*][b]name[/b]: up to 48 bytes, no [b]|[/b], [b];[/b] or [b]#[/b]. Without a name line the file name is used.[/*]
[*]Values outside a setting's range are clamped, and 0/1 settings treat anything but 0 as 1.[/*]
[*]Keys not listed below are ignored, so a preset can't change the on/off switches, the shoulder side, banners or key bindings.[/*]
[*]Up to 54 presets are read, in file-name order. Files over 64 KB, or without a single preset key, are skipped. [font=Courier New]UE4SS.log[/font] lists each preset it loaded.[/*]
[/list]

[b]Keys[/b] (range, shipped default)
[list]
[*]Follow: [font=Courier New]follow_rate_h[/font] and [font=Courier New]follow_rate_v[/font] (0.5 to 30, 6.5 and 10), [font=Courier New]curve_h[/font] and [font=Courier New]curve_v[/font] (0 constant, 1 linear, 2 smoothstep, 3 ease in-out; 2 and 0), [font=Courier New]catchup_distance[/font] (10 to 500 cm, 150), [font=Courier New]min_rate_scale[/font] (0.05 to 1, 0.35), [font=Courier New]max_lag_h[/font] (0 to 300 cm, 85), [font=Courier New]max_lag_v[/font] (0 to 200 cm, 50), [font=Courier New]soft_leash[/font] (0 or 1, 1), [font=Courier New]aiming_follow[/font] (0 to 100 %, 30), [font=Courier New]combat_follow[/font] (0 to 100 %, 100), [font=Courier New]traversal_follow[/font] (0 to 100 %, 100)[/*]
[*]Turning: [font=Courier New]rotation_smoothing[/font] (0 or 1, 0), [font=Courier New]rotation_rate[/font] (1 to 60, 20), [font=Courier New]combat_rotation[/font] (0 to 100 %, 100), [font=Courier New]traversal_rotation[/font] (0 to 100 %, 100)[/*]
[*]Look limits: [font=Courier New]pitch_min[/font] (-89 to -30, -60), [font=Courier New]pitch_max[/font] (20 to 89, 40); the modes shipped at -60 / 40 only[/*]
[*]Per situation, with the prefix [font=Courier New]exploration_[/font], [font=Courier New]sprint_[/font], [font=Courier New]combat_[/font], [font=Courier New]focus_[/font], [font=Courier New]aiming_[/font] or [font=Courier New]traversal_[/font]: [font=Courier New]distance[/font] (25 to 250 %, 100), [font=Courier New]height[/font] (-50 to 100 cm, 0), [font=Courier New]shoulder[/font] (-60 to 100 cm, 0; not for traversal), [font=Courier New]fov[/font] (-30 to 30 degrees, 0)[/*]
[/list]

[b]Sharing a preset on Nexus[/b]
Pack the file with its folder path, [i]ue4ss\Mods\DWSmoothwalker\config\presets\YourName - Low Chase.ini[/i], so players extract it into [i]Dawnwalker\Binaries\Win64[/i] like Smoothwalker itself. List Smoothwalker as a requirement, and pick a file name no other preset is likely to use; same-named files replace each other on extract.[/spoiler]

[size=4][b]For mod authors: the camera API[/b][/size]
[spoiler]
Smoothwalker puts a [font=Courier New]Smoothwalker[/font] table into every UE4SS Lua mod's state before the script's top level runs, so there is nothing to require or copy. [font=Courier New]Smoothwalker == nil[/font] means it is not installed; check [font=Courier New]api_version[/font] (4 in this release, it only grows).

[list]
[*][font=Courier New]view()[/font], [font=Courier New]live()[/font], [font=Courier New]enabled()[/font]: the camera as the game built it and as shown, the pivot, and whether updates are live. Callable from any thread.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]layer_set{ offset, rotation, fov, fov_abs, weight, blend, ttl }[/font], [font=Courier New]layer_clear()[/font]: one layer per mod, eight slots, applied after the follow. A lease ([font=Courier New]ttl[/font]) fades it out if your mod stops refreshing it.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]claim{ keep_layers, ttl }[/font], [font=Courier New]release("cut" or "glide")[/font], [font=Courier New]owner()[/font]: one owner at a time takes the camera over; on release Smoothwalker cuts or glides back to its follow.[/*]
[/list]
The write calls are game-thread only and answer [font=Courier New]nil, reason[/font] instead of throwing. The full reference and the threading rules are in [url=https://github.com/littleRabbit94/dw-smoothwalker/blob/main/docs/api.md]docs/api.md[/url] on GitHub. The optional file [b]Smoothwalker Example API Mod[/b] (Files tab, also [url=https://github.com/littleRabbit94/dw-smoothwalker/tree/main/release/example-consumer]on GitHub[/url]) is a complete drop-in Lua mod using it: F9 a zoom layer, F10 a dutch angle, F11 a claim with a glide back, and a console command that prints the reads. Copy from it freely.[/spoiler]

[size=4][b]Source[/b][/size]
Source is on [url=https://github.com/littleRabbit94/dw-smoothwalker]GitHub[/url].
For bug reports, the DWSmoothwalker lines from your [font=Courier New]UE4SS.log[/font], your game build and which UE4SS package you run should be enough.

